## 1. Introduction
Semiconducting perovskite materials based on organoleadtrihalide compounds are promising solution-process light harvesters. Over the past few years, perovskite solar cells (PSCs) have experienced a tremendous interest, moving from a power conversion efficiency (PCE) of $9.7\%^{[1]}$ up to $20.1\%$ . 2 This phenomenon is mainly due to the peculiar properties of the perovskite material which combines a high absorption coefficient with an acceptable mobility of charge carriers. 1,3 That allows the utilization of an ultrathin absorber layer 4–8 leading to a very high PCE.
To reach the theoretically predicted PCE maximum, it is necessary to find an optimal trade-off between high optical absorption and extremely thin absorbers. In fact, while it has been possible to reach high experimental values of short circuit current density $(J_{\mathrm{sc}})$ and open circuit voltage $(V_{\mathrm{oc}})$ , consistent with which are still orders of magnitude longer than the hot carrier cooling (or thermalization) time $_{(\approx0.4}$ ps). 10  Thus, a large amount of the converted photon energy is wasted in the thermalization process and in the carrier trapping. In this context, several materials have been proposed to facilitate electron/ hole extractions, including fullerene, 11  graphene, 12  or core/ shell metal nanoparticles. 13  Although graphene-based materials have been demonstrated to improve the performance and stability of organic and hybrid photovoltaic devices as recently reviewed by Singh and Nalwa, 14,15  a direct evidence is yet to be obtained regarding their role as a fast electron injector rather than just an electron trapping center. 16
In this work, to improve the electron extraction from the perovskite absorber into the mesoporous $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ an additional lithium-neutralized graphene oxide (GO-Li) layer as electron transporting Layer (ETL) is used. GO-Li has been already used as ETL in organic photovoltaic devices by Kakavelakis et al. 17 The authors demonstrated that the replacement of $-\mathrm{H}$ atoms in the carboxyl groups of GO by Li atoms can effectively reduce the working function (WF) of GO from 4.9 to $4.3\pm0.1\ \mathrm{eV}.$ Due to low electronegativity and low WF, Li atoms lose their valence electrons to the GO plane, and the resulting positive $\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ induces dipoles. This transfer of charge from the metal to the GO leads to a shift in the Fermi level toward the vacuum and a consequent decrease in WF. 18
Since the WF of GO-Li displays a good match with the Lowest Unoccupied Molecular Orbital (LUMO) level of mesoporous $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ in this work a new efficient perovskite solar cell structure is proposed which includes the GO-Li as an interlayer between the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and the perovskite harvester. The resulting PSCs exhibit enhanced $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ and FF and reduced
## www.MaterialsViews.com
hysteresis with respect to control devices without the GO-Li ETL. The reported analysis points out the beneficial effects of GO-Li layer on both device electrical performance and longterm stability. In particular the deep analysis of both 1 SUN and in dark current–voltage $(I{-}V)$ characteristics, transient measurements and steady state fluorescence spectroscopy reveal an improved charge extraction/injection at the cell photoelectrode (PE) when GO-Li is used as ETL. At the same time GO-Li layer is demonstrated to passivate the mesoporous $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ oxygen defects/vacancies that act as reactive centers when the device is exposed to the moisture attack. That results in an improved stability and an enlarged device lifetime of the nonencapsulated devices employing GO-Li as ETL, under 1 SUN prolonged stress test.
## 2. Results and Discussion
Two sets (eight cells per type) of PSC structures employing 2,20,7,70-tetrakis- $(N,N$ -dip-methoxyphenylamine) ${\mathfrak{g}},{\mathfrak{g}}^{\prime}$ - spirobifluorene (Spiro-MeOTAD) as hole transporting layer (HTL) were fabricated and compared, namely, reference PSCs composed by $\mathrm{FTO/c.TiO_{2}/m{\cdot}T i O_{2}/}$ perovskite/Spiro-MeOTAD/ Au and PSCs fabricated with GO-Li as ETL onto $\ensuremath{\mathrm{m}}{\cdot}\ensuremath{\mathrm{Ti}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{O}}_{2}$ layer $\mathrm{\small{[FTO/c\cdotTiO_{2}/m\cdot T i O_{2}/G O\cdot L i/}}$ perovskite/Spiro-MeOTAD/Au] as reported in Figure 1A,B, respectively.
The GO-Li chemical and physical properties were widely discussed in a previous work 17  and have been used as starting point to motivate the position of the GO-Li ETL in the device architecture. In fact UPS (Ultraviolet Photoelectron Spectroscopy) measurements exhibited an error of $\pm0.05\ \mathrm{\eV},$ by providing a WF of $4.27\pm0.05\mathrm{~eV}$ for GO-Li, 17  which perfectly match the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ conduction band as schematically showed in the energy band diagram reported in Figure 2  The maximum value of GO-Li WF required to inject electrons into the $\ensuremath{\mathrm{m}}{\cdot}\ensuremath{\mathrm{Ti}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{O}}_{2}$ layer without any energy barrier should be $4.25~\mathrm{eV}$ $(k_{\mathrm{B}}T\approx0.025~\mathrm{eV}$ at RT), which is in the range of the GO-Li WF values measured by UPS.
The $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ energy band diagram has been extracted by refs.  19 and  20   The anatase crystal form of both $\mathrm{c}{\cdot}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{m}{\cdot}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{c{-}T i O_{2}/m{-}T i O_{2}/G O{-}L i}$ substrates was verified by means of resonance Raman spectroscopy (Figure S1, Supporting
Information). 21–23  That, in combination with the absorbance measurements (Figure S2, Supporting Information), ensures a band gap of about $\mathsf{\Omega}_{3.2\mathrm{~eV}}[24]$ for both the analyzed substrates.
The presence of typical D (Disorder) and G (Graphitic) vibrational bands of $\mathbf{G}\mathbf{O}^{[25-27]}$ in the Raman spectrum of $\mathrm{c}{\cdot}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/$ $\mathrm{m{\cdot}T i O}_{2}$ /GO-Li carried out by focusing the laser beam onto the $\mathrm{m{\cdot}T i O}_{2}$ layer confirms the interpenetration of GO-Li in the mesoporous layer.
Perovskite and doped spiro-MeOTAD energy levels were extracted by the recent work of Schulz et al. 28  reporting an energy gap of $3.7\ \mathrm{eV}$ for $\mathrm{CH_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3}}$ perovskite and a highest occupied molecular orbital level of $5.2\ \mathrm{eV}$ for doped spiroMeOTAD layer.
The current density-voltage $\left(J-V\right)$ characteristics of the best performing PSCs at 1 SUN illumination and without encapsulation are reported in Figure 3  while the extracted electrical parameters are reported in Table 1  The results repeatability is checked on eight realized devices per typology; the related electrical parameters and the standard deviation are reported in Figure S3 (Supporting Information).
Notably the best performing device comprising GO-Li layer showed a remarkable improvement in PCE $(+12\%)$ in comparison with the performance of the reference sample. In particular the PCE improvement can be related to an increase of $\mathrm{\Delta}J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ $(+10.5\%)$ and the FF $(+7.5\%)$ while the $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ undergoes a slight decrease $(-6\%)$ . The small $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ change is consistent with the fact that the main role of the ultrathin GO-Li layer is to facilitate the electron injection into the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layer. 14  Moreover, the addition of free lithium ions into the electrolyte solution of dye sensitized solar cells is well known to insert deeper surface states below the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ conduction band (CB) edge. 29  The adsorption of lithium cations onto the mesoporous $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ surface leads to a downward displace of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ CB that adversely affects the $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ Thus, it is reasonable to expect a similar $\ensuremath{\mathrm{m}}{\cdot}\ensuremath{\mathrm{Ti}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{O}}_{2}$ CB downshift effect upon the addition of the GO-Li interlayer, resulting in a lowering of the devices $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ The results repeatability has been proved by testing eight devices for each type of PSC. An average PCE improvement of about $12\%$ was found for the PSC set with GO-Li ETL.
In order to explain why the GO-Li insertion is beneficial for the devices performance and in particular for the $J_{\mathrm{SC}},$ a series of electro-optical measurements have been carried out onto the nonencapsulated devices. Figure 4 shows the incident photon-to-current conversion efficiency (IPCE) spectra in the visible wavelength region (from 300 to $800\ \mathrm{nm}$ ) for both the investigated devices. The reported integrated photocurrent density of IPCE spectra exhibit values which are in agreement (percentage difference of $0.98\%$ and $2.6\%$ for best performing GO-Li based and reference devices respectively) with the corresponding values measured under sun simulator. It is worth noting that the overall IPCE improvement is localized in the long wavelength spectral region, between 500 and $780\ \mathrm{nm}$ . Since the absorption of GO-Li thin layer is expected to be small, the IPCE enhancement can likely stem from a better injection of electrons generated by long-wavelength part of the excitation spectrum. In fact, the longer wavelength region corresponds to the low energy photogenerated electrons that could be easy trapped into the perovskite layer prior to be injected into the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layer. Thus, the insertion of GO-Li aids the electron extraction at the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/$ perovskite interface by improving the integrated photocurrent density.
To further support this observation, steady state fluorescence measurement on photoelectrodes (PEs) fabricated with and without the GO-Li layer has been performed (Figure 5). Under the same experimental condition, the $\mathrm{FTO/TiO_{2}/p e r}.$ ovskite and $\mathrm{FTO/TiO_{2}/G O{-}L i_{/}}$ perovskite samples demonstrate a photoluminescence (PL) emission at $760~\mathrm{nm}$ which is typical of the perovskite layer. Notably, when GO-Li is inserted in the PE, the perovskite PL is dramatically quenched (by nearly $55\%$ . Since the PL emission stems from the radiative recombination in perovskite layer, a reduced emission is symptomatic of an enhanced electron injection in the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layer, by further confirming the role of GO-Li layer in enhancing the electrons extraction.
GO-Li passivation effect of $\mathrm{m{\cdot}T i O}_{2}$ surface cannot, however, be immediately excluded. First of all, the passivation effect of GO-Li onto the $\mathrm{m}{\cdot}\mathrm{Ti}{\mathrm{O}}_{2}$ oxygen vacancies can be clearly detected by applying Raman spectroscopy on the glass-F $\mathrm{\ddot{\prime}T O/c\mathrm{-}T i O_{2}/m\mathrm{-}T i O_{2}}$ substrate before and after the deposition of the GO-Li layer (Figure S2 in the Supporting Information). In fact, the presence of oxygen vacancies results in a broadening of the typical anatase $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ vibrational bands due
## www.MaterialsViews.com
to a degradation of crystallinity and in a shift of the main $\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{g}(1)}$ band $(145~\mathrm{cm}^{-1})$ toward higher wavenumbers. 30  The shift from 149 to $145~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and the decreased full width half maximum (FWHM) of the $\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{g}(1)}$ mode after the intercalation of GO-Li layer onto the $\ensuremath{\mathrm{m}}{\cdot}\ensuremath{\mathrm{TiO}_{2}}$ detailed in Table S1 (Supporting Information) give a clear evidence of the oxygen vacancies passivation effect by GO-Li insertion. Furthermore, in a complete device $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ defects passivation could reduce the trap assisted recombination during the charge extraction at the negative electrode, by further enhancing the $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ At this purpose, dark $I{-}V$ and transient measurements have been carried out by employing the Arkeo setup described in the Experimental Section. The in dark $I{-}V$ measurement provides useful information about the recombination processes occurring in the devices. 31  In fact, when the diode model is employed to fit the in dark $I{-}V$ curve, the ideality factor $n$ can be easily extracted. In absence of recombination, $n$ should be equal to the unity, whereas trap-assisted recombination can change it up to 2. 32,33  Figure 6 shows the in dark I–V curves for the best PSCs with and without GO-Li, together with the series resistance $\left(R_{\mathrm{s}}\right)$ and $n$ factor values extracted by the nonlinear curve-fitting procedure.
The raw data and the fitted curves for both reference and GO-Li based samples are plotted in Figure S4A,B (Supporting Information), respectively, for the voltage value between 0.1 and $1\mathrm{~V~}$ where the fitting has been performed. As expected for perovskite based solar cell, n extracted values are significantly larger than 1 for both tested devices, demonstrating that trap assisted recombination plays a substantial role in the recombination process. 34  However, PSC with the GO-Li ETL showed an $n$ factor and a $R_{\mathrm{S}}$ smaller with respect to the reference PSC, suggesting a decreased trap state density at $\mathrm{TiO}_{2I}$ /perovskite interface when GO-Li is added. Mesoporous $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ is well known to contain many oxygen vacancies, in particular at the surface 35,36  by generating electrons trap sites at $\mathrm{\approx}1~\mathrm{eV}$ below the conduction band edge. 37  Thus, a not negligible fraction of the electrons injected into $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ are trapped on these sites and can recombine with holes in the spiro-OMeTAD. 38  On the contrary, when $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ is treated with GO-Li, the residual oxygen moiety of
GO will passivate the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layer enhancing photocatalytic properties because of the increase in the transport efficiency of the photogenerated electrons. 39
The capability of GO-Li to reduce trap assisted recombination is finally demonstrated by a remarkable decrease in the $J{-}V$ curve hysteresis. Figure 7 shows the forward and reverse $J{-}V$ scans for both tested devices, by reporting the hysteresis index calculated as defined by Sanchez et al. 40  As evident, both tested devices presented a marked hysteric behavior but, notably, the insertion of GO-Li in the cell structure reduces the hysteresis factor by $50\%$ . The origin of hysteresis phenomenon in perovskite solar cell is nowadays not completely understood but several explanations have been proposed and supported by theoretical studies. 41  In particular the hysteresis phenomenon is suspected to be related to the perovskite interface properties. 40,42  Very recently, van Reenen et al. 43  modeled the hysterisis by including ion migration phenomena and electronic charge traps, by finally achieving a good agreement with the experimental data. The authors demonstrated that trapped electronic charges recombine with oppositely charged free electronic carriers, with their density to be dependent on the bias-dependent ion distribution in the perovskite. In this context, the ions movement inside the perovskite layer strongly depends on the structural properties of the sensitizer, while the carrier trapping efficiency is related to the effective presence of deep trap states in particular at the perovskite/transporting layer interface. Thus, we can confidently postulate that the passivation effect of GO-Li onto the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ traps can consistently reduce the amount of trapped electrons by reducing the hysteresis index.
Finally, to conclude the analysis of the recombination phenomena, open circuit photovoltage decay (OCVD) measurements under large perturbation regime have been carried out. The debate on the transient measurements interpretation for perovskite solar cell is still open. A commonly accepted conclusion is that the response time derived from OCVD measurement cannot be generally interpreted as a recombination lifetime because other types of relaxation behavior influence the decay of the photovoltage. 40  In our case, tested devices showed a very similar $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ exponential decay (Figure 8). However, by extracting the average time decay constant (provided by a biexponential fitting 44  of the $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ decay) the GO-Li based samples showed a slightly decreased time consistent with the decreased $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ under 1 SUN illumination (Figure 3) and even with the increased recombination current at $1\mathrm{~V~}$ under dark condition (Figure 6). The slightly faster decay time constant $\tau_{\mathrm{b}}$ extracted in the case of GO-Li based samples confirms that the unfavorable $\mathrm{m{\cdot}T i O}_{2}$ CB downshift due to the GO-Li presence overwhelms the advantageous passivation effects of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ oxygen vacancies. This confirms that the GO-Li enhancement effect is mainly attributed to the improved charge injection at the electrode rather than to the decreased charge recombination efficiency.
In order to finally confirm the remarkable improvement of the charge extraction in GO-Li PSCs, the $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ of both investigated devices has been recorded by progressively increasing the irradiation level $(P_{\mathrm{inc}})$ up to $0.18\mathrm{~mW~cm}^{-2}$ You et al. 45 employed this method to detect the presence of energy barrier in perovskite devices. In fact, a quite nonlinear trend of $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ versus light intensity is indicative of the presence in the device of energy barriers affecting the charge extraction process. Figure 9 shows the $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ versus $P_{\mathrm{inc}}$ for both the best performing devices (curves a and b for device without and with GO-Li, respectively) and the $R^{2}$ parameter extracted for both linear fitted curves. Notably, the use of GO-Li as ETL improves the $R^{2}$ of fitted curve, by showing a more linear trend of $J_{\mathrm{SC}}{-}P_{\mathrm{inc}}$ curve when compared with reference sample.
Thus, it is possible to conclude that the insertion of GO-Li as interlayer ETL in perovskite solar cell has the effect to boost the overall efficiency by enhancing both the device’s $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ and the FF. In fact, the GO-Li insertion has been demonstrated to improve the electron extraction at the PE by retaining a good balance between holes and electrons extraction at the PE. On the contrary, the $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ value undergoes an overall decrease due to the downward shift of $\mathrm{m{\cdot}T i O}_{2}$ CB only partially balanced by the passivation of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ surface defect. Nevertheless, the losing in $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ is largely compensated by the increase in $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ finally leading to an increased efficiency.
Finally, the stability of the device is tested under prolonged illumination condition $(100\mathrm{mW}\mathrm{cm}^{-2})$ provided by a white LED at room temperature, as described in the Experimental Section. $J{-}V$ curves, in both reverse and forward scan direction, were acquired every $6~\mathrm{min}$ during the aging time and electrical parameters were extracted for each $J{-}V$ curve. Dark $J{-}V$ characteristics, OCVD transient analysis, and the light dependent measurements $(J_{\mathsf{S C}}$ vs light incident power) were recorded at the beginning and at the end of the stress test. Cells were not encapsulated.
The degradation trends of normalized electrical parameters are reported in Figure 10
The efficiency decrease is mainly determined by losses in $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ (Figure 10A) and FF (Figure 10C) for both tested devices but with different degradation rates. Surprisingly, the device with
GO-Li ETL shows an improved stability under the prolonged light soaking test than the reference device. The same results have been obtained by examining the electrical parameters trends extracted by the forward scan $J{-}V$ characteristics (here not reported).
These results clearly show the use of GO-Li ETL as an effective pathway toward the improvement of the perovskite solar cell stability, even though further investigations are needed to clarify the effective role of GO-Li in slowing down the degradation processes. The main degradation phenomenon for PSC involves the doped spiro-MeOTAD and the perovskite active layers. As recently reported in our previous work, 46  the replacement of doped spiro-MeOTAD with organic hole conductor layer such as phthalocyanine derivative could improve the devices stability. In fact, spiro-MeOTAD, in its pristine form, suffers from low hole mobility and low conductivity, and dopants are needed to improve this effect. Usually tertbutylpyridine is added to the spiro-MeOTAD solution to obtain a significant improvement in the $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ while Lithium Bis (Trifluoromethanesulfonyl)Imide (LiTFSI) is added to improve the $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ . 47  These additives are well known to reduce the device’s stability. Hawash et al. 48  demonstrated that the diffusion of the dopant upon air and light exposure causes the slowing down of hole mobility and finally degrades the perovskite active layer.
Thus, most likely the degrading process involves both the hole transporting material (HTM) and the sensitizer by gradually slowing down the hole collection efficiency of HTL and the generation of free charges in the perovskite active layer.
Leijtens et al. 38  proposed a detailed degradation mechanism arising from light-induced desorption of surface-adsorbed oxygen. In fact, due to the presence of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ oxygen vacancies, oxygen radical can be adsorbed on $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ surface and reacts with the photogenerated holes in $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layer. The oxygen is thus released by leaving a deep electron trap state; the consequently trapped electron can recombine with the excess of holes in the doped-hole transporter. This phenomenon is supposed to accelerate during the aging test that causes radical oxygen adsorption/ release at $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ vacancies and a slowing down of hole mobility in the HTL due to the abovementioned spiro-MeOTAD degradation. Consequently, our results strongly suggest that the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ traps are passivated by the GO-Li ETL enhancing the stability of the PSCs.
## 3. Conclusions
This work reports a new efficient ETL for mesoscopic PSCs. In particular, GO-Li has been inserted in a typical device structure between the sensitizer and $\ensuremath{\mathrm{m}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{TiO}_{2}}$ layer. Electrical, optical and transient analysis demonstrated the main effect of GO-Li in improving the electron injection from the sensitizer to the $\mathrm{m{\cdot}T i O}_{2}$ layer by remarkably increasing the $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ $(+10.5\%)$ . Furthermore, the good balance between electrons and holes extraction at the electrodes resulted in an increased FF $(+7.5\%)$ while a better injection of electrons generated by long-wavelength part of the excitation spectrum results in a broadened IPCE spectrum. Moreover, a reduction of about $50\%$ in devices $J{-}V$ curves hysteresis demonstrated the capability of GO-Li layer to reduce trap assisted recombination at $\mathrm{m}{\cdot}\mathrm{Ti}{\mathrm{O}}_{2}$ /perovskite interface. Notably the tested devices showed an improved stability when subjected, without encapsulation, to prolonged illumination aging test. The physical mechanism for an increased stability of GO-Li based device is yet under investigation. Nevertheless, the reported analysis suggests that the enhanced device’s stability stems mainly from the passivation of $\mathrm{m}{\cdot}\mathrm{Ti}{\mathrm{O}}_{2}$ oxygen vacancies that are the main reaction centers for moisture attack. The relevant results underline how the use of a facile WF tuning of graphene-based material can provide optimized efficiency and improve the lifetime of PSCs. Furthermore, the innovative application of GO-Li in organoleadtrihalide PSCs together with the employed vacuum and vapor assisted technique open a viable route to improve further the stability of the device by retaining this technology repeatable and suitable for next large area applications.
## 4. Experimental Section
Material—Preparation of Graphene Oxide: GO was prepared from graphite powder (Alfa Aesar. ${\approx}200$ mesh) according to a modified Hummers’ method. 49  In more detail, graphite powder $_{(1.0\mathrm{~g)~}}$ was placed into a mixture of ${\sf H}_{2}{\sf S}{\sf O}_{4}$ $(23\mathrm{~mL},98\%)$ and $N a N O_{3}$ $(0.5~\mathrm{g})$ . The mixture was then stirred and cooled in an ice bath. While maintaining vigorous stirring, $K M n O_{4}$ $(3.0\ g)$ was then added in portions over a period of $2\ h$ . The reaction mixture was left for $4\ h$ in order to reach room temperature before being heated to $35^{\circ}C$ for $30~\mathrm{min}$ . It was then poured into a flask containing deionized water $(50~\mathsf{m L})$ and further heated to $70^{\circ}C$ for $15\mathrm{\min}$ . The mixture was then decanted into $250~\mathrm{mL}$ of deionized water and the unreacted $K M n O_{4}$ was removed by adding $3\%H_{2}O_{2}$ . The reaction mixture was then allowed to settle and decanted. The graphite oxide obtained was then purified by repeated centrifugation and redispersed in deionized water until a negative reaction on sulphate ion (with $\mathsf{B a}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2})$ was achieved. Finally, the resulting GO was dried at $60^{\circ}\mathsf C$ in a vacuum oven for $48\mathrm{~h~}$ before use.
Material—Preparation of Graphene Oxide Functionalized with Lithium: The chemical functionalization of GO with lithium was performed using an aqueous solution of the previous prepared GO $1.5\mathsf{m g}\mathsf{m}\mathsf{L}^{-1}$ , $40~\mathrm{mL}$ ). ${\mathsf{L i}}_{2}{\mathsf{C O}}_{3}$ $(200~\mathsf{m g})$ was then added and the solution was stirred for $1\ h$ . Polyvinylidene difluoride (PVDF) transfer membrane $(0.45~{\upmu\mathrm{m}})$ was used to filter and collect the solid, which was then dissolved in water $(20~\mathsf{m L})$ . The process of dissolution and filtration was repeated two times. The prepared GO-Li was dissolved in water:ethanol (1:3). 50
Perovskite Solar Cells Realization: Solar cells with an active area of $0.1\mathsf{c m}^{2}$ were prepared on fluorine-doped tin oxide (FTO) conductive glass (Pilkington, $8~\Omega~\Pi^{-1}$ , $25~{\mathsf{m m}}\times25~{\mathsf{m m}}$ ) patterned through raster scanning laser $\langle\lambda=1064\:\mathsf{n m}$ , ${\mathsf{N d}}{\mathrm{:YVO}}_{4}$ pulsed at $30~\mathsf{k H z}$ , average output power $P=10$ W). The patterned substrates were cleaned in an ultrasonic bath, using detergent with deionized water, acetone, and 2-propanol (10 min for each step). A patterned compact $\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ $(c-T i O_{2})$ layer was deposited onto the patterned FTO by spray pyrolysis deposition. To measure the final thickness of the $\mathsf{c}_{-}\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ a Dektak Veeco 150 profilometer was used; the thickness of this layer was measured about $70\ \mathsf{n m}$ and verified by SEM image reported in Figure S5 (Supporting Information). Patterning of the $\mathsf{c}{\mathsf{-}}\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ was achieved using a screen printed metal mask which was removed after the $\mathsf{c}{\mathsf{-}}\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ deposition using an HCl-based acidic solution and deionized water. A $350\ n m$ nanocrystalline mesoporous $\mathsf{m}{\cdot}\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ layer (18NR-T paste, Dyesol, diluted with terpineol and ethyl cellulose) was screen-printed onto the $\mathsf{c}_{-}\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ surface and successively sintered at $500~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $30\mathrm{\min}$ . The perovskite active layer deposition was conducted by double step method; a lead iodide solution $(\mathsf{P b l}_{2}$ in $N,N\mathrm{\Omega}$ -dimethylformamide, $460\ m g\ m L^{-1}$ heated at $70~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ was spincoated at 6000 rpm for $\boldsymbol{\mathsf{10}}\boldsymbol{\mathsf{s}}$ and then heated at $80^{\circ}C$ for $\rceil\mathfrak{h}$ in glove-box environment. ${\mathsf{C H}}_{3}{\mathsf{N H}}_{3}{\mathsf{P b l}}_{3}$ crystallization was achieved by employing the vapor assisted deposition technique proposed by Chen et al. 51  and improved by means of vacuum environment as extensively reported by Casaluci et al. 52  More in detail, the $\mathsf{P b l}_{2}$ samples were placed over a hot plate at $\mathsf{l}50^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for about $\textsf{l h}$ and the methylammonium iodide powder was spread around the samples; during this process, the samples and the powder were retained under low vacuum (desiccator lid attached to an Edwards 5 E2M5 Rotary Vane Dual Stage Mechanical Vacuum Pump). The complete conversion of $\mathsf{P b l}_{2}$ layer into ${\mathsf{C H}}_{3}{\mathsf{N H}}_{3}{\mathsf{P b l}}_{3}$ was confirmed by Energy Dispersive X-ray Spectroscopy (X-EDS) analysis reported in Figure S4 (Supporting Information).
Finally, a solution of 2,20,7,70-tetrakis-(N,N-dip-methoxyphenylamine) $^{9,9^{\prime}}$ -spirobifluorene (Spiro-MeOTAD, $67.4\times10^{-3}\mathrm{~\r~{~M~}~}$ doped with tert-butylpyridine $(26\times70^{-3}\mathrm{~~\normalfont~{~M~}~})$ , and Lithium Bis (Trifluoromethanesulfonyl)Imide (Li-TFSI, $55\times10^{-3}~\mathrm{m})$ was spin-coated (2000 rpm for 40 s) in inert atmosphere on perovskite layer. A thickness of about $140\ n m$ for the Spiro-MeOTAD layer was extracted by crosssection SEM image reported in Figure S5 (Supporting Information). The final devices were left in air overnight in a closed box containing silica desiccant prior to realizing the gold contacts $[700\mathsf{n m}]$ by means of high vacuum thermal evaporation.
The GO-Li layer was spin-coated in inert atmosphere (2000 rpm, 60 s) on sintered $\mathsf{m}{\cdot}\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ layer and then heated at $80~^{\circ}C$ for $30~\mathsf{m i n}$ in air. The GO-Li intercalation into the $\mathsf{m}{\cdot}\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ was confirmed by Raman spectrum (curve b) reported in Figure S1 (Supporting Information). Furthermore, the GO-Li deposition over the $\mathsf{m}{\cdot}\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ has been demonstrated to have a negligible effect into the $\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ substrate by absorbance measurement reported in Figure S2 (Supporting Information). The final devices were not encapsulated.
Characterizations: The UV–vis absorption spectra were recorded using UV–vis 2550 Spectrophotometer from Shimadzu. The spectra were collected over a scan range from 400 to $800~\mathsf{n m}$ at a scan rate of $480\ \mathsf{n m\min^{-1}}$ and a resolution of 1 nm.
Steady-state fluorescence experiments were carried out on a Fluoromax spectrofluorimeter (Jobin–Yvon) operating in the singlephoton counting mode.
Raman spectra were acquired by using a Jobin–Yvon–Horiba microRaman system (LabRAM ARAMIS) equipped with $\mathsf{A r^{+}}$ ion laser $(574\mathsf{n m})$ as the excitation source. The cut-off from the notch filters in the spectrometer is less than $\mathsf{100~c m^{-1}}$ . The laser light reached the sample surface at normal incidence by means of ultralong working distance $(50\times)$ objective with $10.5~\mathsf{m m}$ focal distance. The scattered radiation was collected in a backscattering geometry by a spectrometer equipped with a diffraction grating of 1800 lines per mm coupled to a chargecoupled device (CCD) camera. Spectral deconvolution was carried out by nonlinear least-squares fitting of the Raman peaks to a mixture of Lorentzian and Gaussian line shapes, providing the peak position, width, height, and integrated intensity of each Raman band.
Masked devices were tested in air atmosphere without encapsulation under a solar simulator (ABET Sun 2000, class A) at AM1.5 and $100m\aleph c m^{-2}$ illumination conditions, calibrated with a certified reference Si Cell (RERA Solutions RR-1002). Incident power was measured whit a Skye SKS 1110 sensor. The class was measured with a BLACK-Comet UV–vis spectrometer.
IPCE spectra acquisition were carried out by means of a homemade setup composed by a monochromator (Newport, mod. 74000) coupled with a xenon lamp (Oriel Apex, Newport) and a source meter (Keithley, mod. 2612). A home-made LabVIEW program controls the spectra acquisition.
## www.MaterialsViews.com
The measurements involved in the stability test (JV, dark-JV, transient photovoltage, and variable incident power) were performed with a customized PXI (National Instruments)-based platform (Arkeo). The apparatus is composed of a high speed 4 channel source-meter with remote sensing (NI PXIe 4141) and a programmable power supply (NI PXIe 4112) used to drive a high power white led (Bridgelux-50C10K0 $@$ 5000 Kelvin). Equivalent incident power of 1 SUN $(700\ m\times100^{-2})$ was calibrated through the Mismatch Factor with a certified reference Si cell (RERA Solutions RR-1002).
The thicknesses of the constituent layers and the X-EDS profiles were evaluated from cross-sectional scanning electron microscopy micrographs (FE-SEM Leo Supra 35) equipped with an INCAx-SightOxford Instruments X-EDS.
